WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.767
-Our first guest tonight is
an Emmy-nominated actress

00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:05.634
for her work on
"Orange is the New Black."

00:00:05.634 --> 00:00:07.667
She stars in the
limited series "Pam &amp; Tommy,"

00:00:07.667 --> 00:00:10.033
which is now streaming in
its entirety on Hulu.

00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:11.934
Please welcome back to the show
one of our favorites,

00:00:11.934 --> 00:00:13.701
Taylor Schilling, everyone!

00:00:13.701 --> 00:00:17.267
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.601
Welcome back.

00:00:32.601 --> 00:00:33.934
-I always feel like
I'm seeing my best friend.

00:00:33.934 --> 00:00:35.434
My best TV friend.

00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:38.467
-I mean, you're one of our more
frequent guests on this show.

00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:40.934
But it's been three years

00:00:40.934 --> 00:00:42.067
since we've seen
each other in person.

00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:43.100
We've done some Zoom,

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.367
but it's great to see you
in person again.

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:47.901
-Yeah.
-Okay.

00:00:47.901 --> 00:00:50.100
As always,
you have a way with words.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:51.667
-Yeah. Thank you.

00:00:51.667 --> 00:00:53.434
-One of the things we
talked about during the pandemic

00:00:53.434 --> 00:00:56.467
is that you started
suffering from narcolepsy,

00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:59.334
which is a very serious
condition that just seems funny
--

00:00:59.334 --> 00:01:00.868
it's funny when
it happens to you.

00:01:00.868 --> 00:01:04.300
-Yeah. I mean, I don't want to
make fun of people who have --

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:05.834
Narcolepsy is awful.

00:01:05.834 --> 00:01:07.234
-Yeah, I can only imagine.

00:01:07.234 --> 00:01:10.534
-I fall asleep a lot.
It's weird. I'm getting older.

00:01:10.534 --> 00:01:11.701
I don't know what's happening
all the time to me.

00:01:11.701 --> 00:01:13.434
-So it is still happening.
-No, no.

00:01:13.434 --> 00:01:16.934
It's less,
except when I'm in cars.

00:01:16.934 --> 00:01:18.534
-That's maybe the worst
time for it to happen.

00:01:18.534 --> 00:01:20.367
-Yeah.
-Not driving.

00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:22.033
-Not driving.
-Okay, good. Okay.

00:01:22.033 --> 00:01:23.567
-When I'm in a passenger seat
or in the back,

00:01:23.567 --> 00:01:27.467
I feel very
relaxed and I fall asleep.

00:01:27.467 --> 00:01:29.000
-I think that's okay, though.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.133
-Yeah, I think it's okay.

00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:32.067
-I think,
again, as we get older,

00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:35.033
I would like to be
sleeping more in general.

00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:38.934
-Yeah. That picture of the bowl
on top of

00:01:38.934 --> 00:01:40.667
-And he's like,
"Oh, everybody will love it."

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:42.968
And I'm like, "You're a bad kid.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.234 --> 00:01:46.200
You're a bad kid.
You're a liar."

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.234
Well, I'm mostly just glad
that you've recovered from that,

00:01:50.234 --> 00:01:51.434
because that must have
been scary.

00:01:51.434 --> 00:01:53.400
-It was. You know, I'm confused.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.133
It's, like, a healthier
coping mechanism than others.

00:01:57.133 --> 00:01:58.601
Do you know what I mean?
-Oh, that's interesting. Yeah.

00:01:58.601 --> 00:02:03.234
-I think it's I'd I prefer that
if I'm like, "Okay, crisis,"

00:02:03.234 --> 00:02:07.734
I fall asleep, as opposed to
doing something else.

00:02:07.734 --> 00:02:10.634
-Yeah. Well,
I guess it's a way of, like,

00:02:10.634 --> 00:02:13.100
shutting you down
before you can maybe, like,

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.100
do your worst instincts.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.734
-Right. Yeah.
Exactly. Exactly.

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:16.868
It's helpful.

00:02:16.868 --> 00:02:18.734
I don't have time for my
worst instincts anymore.

00:02:18.734 --> 00:02:19.834
-No. It's just better to
sleep it off.

00:02:19.834 --> 00:02:21.767
-I'm an adult. Yeah.

00:02:21.767 --> 00:02:23.267
-You're of an adult back
in New York City.

00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:25.868
We've talked a lot about
when you were younger.

00:02:25.868 --> 00:02:28.367
Your early apartments
in New York were pretty gnarly.

00:02:28.367 --> 00:02:29.667
And now you're back.

00:02:29.667 --> 00:02:31.300
You know, obviously,
your career has been met with

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:32.534
a fair amount of success.

00:02:32.534 --> 00:02:34.667
I'm assuming you've got
a great pad right now.

00:02:34.667 --> 00:02:36.934
-Yeah.
[ Laughs ]

00:02:36.934 --> 00:02:40.300
Yeah, so, I was --
Yeah, so, It's been

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:42.234
a hard transition for me
back to New York.

00:02:42.234 --> 00:02:44.334
-Okay.
-I got to my apartment.

00:02:44.334 --> 00:02:48.968
I thought -- You know, in the
theme of trying to work against

00:02:48.968 --> 00:02:53.834
my lower instincts,
I thought, "It's 3:30.

00:02:53.834 --> 00:02:55.567
I'll take a bath."

00:02:55.567 --> 00:02:59.501
You know, I put the dog --
I gave the dog water.

00:02:59.501 --> 00:03:01.801
I'm going to drink some water.
It's been a long time.

00:03:01.801 --> 00:03:05.667
-Since you've had water?
-Yes! Well, yes.

00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:08.100
It's been a long time since
I've been in a studio.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.434
-Oh, interesting.

00:03:10.434 --> 00:03:11.868
-So --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:17.234
So, I was like, "Okay,
I'm going to take a bath."

00:03:17.234 --> 00:03:19.701
And I had -- I ran a bath.

00:03:19.701 --> 00:03:22.334
I was like -- I took 10 minutes
and I thought,

00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:24.467
"Okay, I'll go away for
10 minutes,

00:03:24.467 --> 00:03:26.100
and the bath will fill up."

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.767
And then, I got back,

00:03:27.767 --> 00:03:29.234
and it was cold water.

00:03:29.234 --> 00:03:30.968
So I was like, "[Bleep], this
place doesn't have hot water.

00:03:30.968 --> 00:03:32.834
Okay, whatever.
You know, New York."

00:03:32.834 --> 00:03:33.968
-Right.

00:03:33.968 --> 00:03:37.400
-And I thought, "Alright."
And I drained it.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:40.200
And then I was
like, "What the [bleep]"

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.501
And I was like, "Did I leave
a faucet on downstairs?"

00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:47.634
And then I walked downstairs,
and the water was --

00:03:47.634 --> 00:03:50.934
The bathtub was draining through
a light fixture

00:03:50.934 --> 00:03:52.667
on the first floor.

00:03:52.667 --> 00:03:57.367
And I thought,
"I can't live this way."

00:03:57.367 --> 00:04:00.467
But I've been living
this way my whole life.

00:04:00.467 --> 00:04:01.534
It doesn't matter.

00:04:01.534 --> 00:04:03.467
It doesn't matter
what the apartment --

00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:05.601
And it's a nice place.
-It's a nice place.

00:04:05.601 --> 00:04:08.167
Can I make one -- Please don't
take this as a criticism.

00:04:08.167 --> 00:04:09.734
-Oh, God.
What should I have done?

00:04:09.734 --> 00:04:11.834
-Well, I think when
you're like, "I ran a bath,

00:04:11.834 --> 00:04:14.067
and I gave it 10 minutes,"

00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:17.200
I think that's too much
time to leave a bath alone.

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:18.667
-Oh, [bleep]
-Yeah.

00:04:18.667 --> 00:04:20.834
I mean, I think that,
more often than not,

00:04:20.834 --> 00:04:21.801
ends with, like,
water from a light fixture.

00:04:21.801 --> 00:04:23.701
-Problems. Yeah.

00:04:23.701 --> 00:04:26.501
That's actually --
That's a good point.

00:04:26.501 --> 00:04:27.901
Maybe that's too long.

00:04:27.901 --> 00:04:29.467
-Also, you sent us
a video of something

00:04:29.467 --> 00:04:30.567
that happened in your apartment.

00:04:30.567 --> 00:04:34.133
And I would say
I'm pretty easygoing.

00:04:34.133 --> 00:04:36.100
I think if stuff goes wrong
in an apartment,

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.534
I could live with it
pretty well.

00:04:37.534 --> 00:04:39.334
I'm not sure I'd be able to
handle this.

00:04:39.334 --> 00:04:40.501
-Yeah.

00:04:40.501 --> 00:04:42.300
-And your dog is in this
video, as well, Tank.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.400
-Yeah.
-Tank seems okay with it.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.868
-Tank is like, "Why?
Why do I have to --"

00:04:47.868 --> 00:04:50.133
-I like to think you're making
a video of this,

00:04:50.133 --> 00:04:51.834
and Tank is looking at you,
saying,

00:04:51.834 --> 00:04:53.100
"You need to get on the phone

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.167
with who you rented
this place from."

00:04:54.167 --> 00:04:56.334
-Yes.
-Let's take a look at the video.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:01.868
-[ Chuckles ] Tank.

00:05:01.868 --> 00:05:04.701
-So, those are just the lights.

00:05:05.834 --> 00:05:07.067
That's what the lights
were doing.

00:05:07.067 --> 00:05:09.167
-She looked so scared.

00:05:09.167 --> 00:05:10.801
-Well, who wouldn't look scared?

00:05:10.801 --> 00:05:11.901
-It's so crazy.

00:05:11.901 --> 00:05:13.501
I wanted to make sure
that they knew.

00:05:13.501 --> 00:05:16.000
Well, okay, so, the water
came through the light fixture,

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.801
and then I tried again
to take a bath.

00:05:17.801 --> 00:05:19.200
And then they had fixed it,

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:20.968
and then the water
came through a closet.

00:05:20.968 --> 00:05:23.367
And then, later that night,

00:05:23.367 --> 00:05:25.334
I tried to use
the dimmer switch,

00:05:25.334 --> 00:05:26.701
and that started happening.

00:05:28.334 --> 00:05:29.501
I'm still there.

00:05:29.501 --> 00:05:31.000
-You're still in the apartment?
-Yeah.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:34.000
-What do you think the breaking
point will be with the place?

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:35.667
-I don't know. I don't know.
-Yeah.

00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:37.067
They must think you're the
best tenant they've ever had.

00:05:37.067 --> 00:05:40.100
-I guess so.
-How is Tank, in general?

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:41.367
-She's doing well.
-Yeah.

00:05:41.367 --> 00:05:46.501
-I think she's aging
into this sort of space of,

00:05:46.501 --> 00:05:49.434
"I understand this world
better than you do."

00:05:49.434 --> 00:05:52.734
Like that vibe of her just
sitting there kind of like,

00:05:52.734 --> 00:05:54.133
"Alright, this is what
you're going to do right now.

00:05:54.133 --> 00:05:55.300
Cool."

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:57.934
-I want to talk about
your fantastic new show.

00:05:57.934 --> 00:05:59.400
Will you stick around, please?
-Of course. Thank you.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:00.667
-Alright, you guys,
we'll be right back

00:06:00.667 --> 00:06:01.801
with more from Taylor Schilling.

